.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt IMP72-CIMPORT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm imp-cimport
.Nd write the IMP72 declarations of the functions, structures and constants of a C header
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n Ar name
.Op Fl p Ar prefix
.Op Fl D Ar macro
.Op Fl I Ar dir
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Ar header
.Nm
.Op Fl n Ar name
.Op Fl p Ar prefix
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Fl i Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the declarations of a C header and writes an IMP72 source fragment: an
.Sy EXTERNAL
statement for each function whose prototype IMP72 can express
.Pq Ql EXTERNAL INT puts(PTR); ,
a
.Sy STRUCT
statement for each structure whose layout it can express, and a
.Sy LET
statement for each enumerator and for each macro named with
.Fl D .
What it cannot express is listed in a comment at the end of the output, each
with its reason.
.Pp
The C preprocessor reads
.Ql #include <header> :
.Ar header
is a header name as the directive writes it
.Pq Ql stdio.h , Ql sys/stat.h ,
and may be written
.Ql <name>
or
.Ql \&"name\&" .
The preprocessor is the command in
.Ev CC
.Pq Ql cc
with
.Fl E .
With
.Fl i
the tool reads text that the preprocessor made instead, from a file or from the
standard input
.Pq Ql - ,
and runs no other program.
.Pp
The types of the declarations are those of
.Pa FFI.md :
.Ql CHAR UCHAR SHORT USHORT INT UINT LONG ULONG LLONG ULLONG SIZE PTR DOUBLE FLOAT
and
.Ql VOID
for a result.
Typedef names are followed through the declarations the tool has read
.Pq Ql size_t
is
.Ql SIZE ;
the others are the type they stand for.
A pointer of any kind, an array parameter and a function pointer are
.Ql PTR ;
.Ql char
is
.Ql CHAR ,
which is signed, also on machines where the C
.Ql char
is unsigned;
an enumeration is
.Ql INT ;
.Ql const ,
.Ql volatile ,
.Ql restrict ,
.Ql static ,
.Ql extern ,
.Ql inline ,
.Ql __extension__
and attributes are ignored.
A function of a variable number of arguments ends its parameters with
.Ql ... ;
a function declared with empty parentheses is
.Ql (...) .
.Pp
A structure is declared under its tag, or under the typedef name of a structure
without a tag, with the types of its fields, arrays
.Pq Ql CHAR name[20] ,
and structures defined earlier as nested structures.
The declarations are in the order of the header, and a structure that a selected
structure contains is written before it.
IMP72 computes the offsets for the machine the program is compiled for, by the
rules of a C compiler.
.Pp
The output is a fragment of a source file, not a file of its own: a declaration
holds to the end of the compilation unit that has it.
Put it in front of the program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp-cimport -n qsort -n malloc -o decls.imp stdlib.h
cat decls.imp prog.imp > all.imp
imp all.imp
.Ed
.Pp
A name that is a word of the IMP72 grammar
.Pq Ql INT , Ql PRINT , Ql IN
is written between exclamation marks
.Pq Ql !PRINT! ;
names are case sensitive.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n Ar name
Declare only this name: a function, a structure (by tag or by typedef name), a
typedef name of a structure, or an enumerator.
May be repeated.
A name that nothing declares is reported on the standard error.
.It Fl p Ar prefix
Declare the names that start with
.Ar prefix .
May be repeated.
Without
.Fl n
and
.Fl p
every name is declared, including those of the headers the header includes.
.It Fl D Ar macro
Write
.Ql LET macro=value;
for the integer value of a macro.
The C compiler evaluates it: a program that prints the value is compiled and
run in a private directory, and the directory is deleted afterwards.
A negative value is written as its 64-bit pattern, with the signed value in a
comment.
A macro that is not defined or not an integer is listed as not expressed.
May be repeated; needs a header.
.It Fl I Ar dir
A directory for the preprocessor to search, as for the C compiler.
May be repeated.
.It Fl o Ar file
Write the declarations to
.Ar file
instead of the standard output.
.It Fl i Ar file
Read preprocessed text from
.Ar file
.Pq Ql -
for the standard input
instead of running the preprocessor.
.It Fl h
Print the usage.
.El
.Sh WHAT IT CANNOT EXPRESS
Functions with a structure or union argument or result, a
.Vt va_list
argument, a
.Vt long double ,
complex or 128-bit type, or more than 16 parameters, and parameters whose type
is not known.
An unnamed bit field is padding: width 0 writes nothing and a width of whole
bytes writes a member
.Ql UCHAR PADn[bytes] ;
the width may use numbers, enumerators,
.Ql sizeof
of a base type, of a type name or of a pointer,
.Ql ==
and
.Ql !=
(the headers of musl write the padding of
.Vt struct timespec
so).
Structures with a named bit field, an unnamed one whose width cannot be computed, a union member, a member named
.Ql _ , a member whose type cannot be
expressed, a flexible array member, an array length that is not a number or a
sum, difference, product, shift or bitwise or of numbers, a structure defined
inside the structure, or a
.Ql packed
or
.Ql aligned
attribute.
Enumerators whose value is not such an expression.
Variables, function-like macros, and macros not named with
.Fl D
are not declared.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev CC
The C compiler, as the words of a shell command
.Pq Ql cc .
It preprocesses the header, and for
.Fl D
compiles and runs the probe.
.It Ev TMPDIR
The directory for the private directory of the probe of
.Fl D ,
when it is an absolute path naming a directory; otherwise
.Pa /tmp .
The directory is made by
.Xr mkdtemp 3
with mode 0700 and deleted on exit and on
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGTERM .
No other temporary file is made.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
A file could not be read or written, the preprocessor failed, or an argument
was not acceptable.
.It 2
The command line was wrong (the usage is printed).
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The declarations of two functions and a structure of the C library:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp-cimport -n strlen -n memcpy string.h
imp-cimport -n tm -n mktime time.h
.Ed
.Pp
The values of macros:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp-cimport -n puts -D EOF -D BUFSIZ stdio.h
.Ed
.Pp
Text made earlier:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cc -E -x c header.h > header.i
imp-cimport -p mpz_ -i header.i
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr imp 1 ,
.Pa /usr/share/doc/imp/FFI.md
.Sh AUTHORS
.An The imp authors .
